{
  "bullets": [
    {
      "text": "The policy now explicitly covers DuckDuckGo’s broader suite of privacy products, including apps, browser extensions, email protection, and chatbots, rather than focusing solely on the search engine. This matters because users need to understand how their data is handled across all platforms, not just during web searches.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "hunk": "h1",
          "side": "added",
          "quote": "While we started as a search engine, our apps and extensions now offer protection well beyond search, including when browsing the web, reading email, using chatbots, and even using other apps."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The new version clarifies that DuckDuckGo does not log IP addresses or unique identifiers to disk in a way that ties back to individual users, replacing older explanations of technical redirects and proxy workarounds. This is important because it directly addresses modern concerns about metadata tracking and confirms that search activity remains truly anonymous.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "hunk": "h1",
          "side": "added",
          "quote": "we never log IP addresses to disk that could be tied back to you."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The updated policy replaces vague mentions of affiliate commissions with a clear explanation that revenue comes from private search ads based on the search results page rather than personalized user profiles. This matters because it reassures users that their browsing behavior isn’t being sold or used to build targeted advertising profiles.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "hunk": "h1",
          "side": "added",
          "quote": "Since we don’t have that information, search ads on DuckDuckGo are based on the search results page you’re viewing, and not based on you as a person."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The document now explicitly outlines how optional features like Email Protection and Sync & Backup handle limited personal information, emphasizing that users must opt-in and that decryption keys for synced chats remain solely on user devices. This is crucial for transparency around features that do require some user data while maintaining end-to-end encryption guarantees.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "hunk": "h1",
          "side": "added",
          "quote": "the master decryption key exists only on your synced devices, meaning DuckDuckGo cannot decrypt and read your chats in any case."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The revised policy shifts from general warnings about law enforcement subpoenas to a firm statement that DuckDuckGo cannot comply with requests for search or chat histories because they are never collected, while also detailing cross-border data transfer protocols and specific user privacy rights. This matters because it sets realistic expectations for legal compliance and aligns the policy with modern data protection regulations.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "hunk": "h1",
          "side": "added",
          "quote": "Critically, it's not possible for us to provide search, chat, or browsing histories linked to you in response to legal requests because we don't have them."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

